Define for an engine: `define.<engine> name` through `endef` stores an ordinary define
of that name, then enters a phony target of the same name whose recipe hands the body to
the engine's builtin and prints what comes back, as `name:; @$(info $(<engine> ${name}))`
would. The engine word is looked up on the table, so a name with no row in this build is
an error at the define; the target is entered while the makefiles are still being read,
the only time make accepts a new rule, and it never becomes the default goal.

--- a/src/read.c
